  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a drone know it is tilting?",
      "a": "A small chip called an inertial measurement unit holds gyroscopes that sense turning and accelerometers that sense which way is down. It reports the drone's angle many times a second.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an IMU?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a drone flight controller do?",
      "a": "It is the drone's brain: it reads the sensors and the pilot's commands, then uses PID control to set each motor's speed hundreds of times a second, keeping the drone steady.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a flight controller?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a drone turn around?",
      "a": "Two rotors spin clockwise and two anticlockwise. Speeding up one pair and slowing the other leaves a little leftover twist, which rotates the drone on the spot while total lift stays the same.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do drone propellers spin in different directions?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a drone fly forward?",
      "a": "The controller speeds up the back rotors slightly, tilting the drone nose-down. Part of the rotors' push now points backwards, which pushes the drone forwards.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a quadcopter move sideways?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a drone hold its position outdoors?",
      "a": "GPS tells it where it is, and a barometer or downward sensors track its height. If wind pushes it off the spot, the controller tilts it back.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a drone hover in the wind?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Most quadcopters have two propellers spinning clockwise and two anticlockwise so that their turning forces cancel.",
    "Drone flight controllers typically update motor speeds hundreds or thousands of times per second."
  ],
